@@ -384,6 +384,31 @@ The shape is recorded only beside a distribution identity. A registry assembled
 without the loader stays honestly unidentified rather than becoming half-identified
 by a value derived from an object in memory.
 
+### What the project remembers, and what it will not let you remove
+
+Opening a project records, in `.autosk/runtime/v1/index.json`, every distribution its
+registry was built from: the digest and the canonical listing that digest was taken
+over. A task's pin says which digest it needs; this says what that digest consisted
+of, so a later reader can tell whether a candidate tree is that distribution.
+
+It records what a digest **meant**, not the files it covered. It cannot hand an old
+task its old code back — holding the bytes so a pinned task keeps running across an
+update is a separate obligation and is not done here.
+
+A distribution is **referenced** while an open task is pinned to it, and removing a
+referenced one is refused. That set is derived from the tasks every time it is asked
+for, never counted and stored: a tally that drifts from the tasks would eventually
+grant permission to delete something still in use, and two structures obliged to
+agree are exactly the kind of thing that stops agreeing. A closed task holds nothing
+— it will not run again, and its pin stays as history.
+
+Two honest limits. The reference set is read from the store's view taken immediately
+before the cross-process lock, so a task pinned in that window is not seen;
+reconciling under that lock would mean writing task files while holding it, and what
+is being removed is a record rather than code anything is running. And a damaged
+index is an error, never an empty one: reading corruption as "nothing recorded" would
+erase the record of every distribution and then permit deleting them all.
+
 What this is not: it pins bytes on disk. It is not a sandbox, and it says nothing
 about what the code does once imported or what it fetches at runtime.
 
